# Lossy encode per-phase wall-clock baseline (2026-05-18)

## Provenance

- Date: 2026-05-18 UTC
- Hostname: lilith (water-cooled AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 32 threads, 128 GB RAM)
- Parent commit: 45445443 (W19-2 libjxl diff review investigation)
- Workspace: ~/work/zen/jxl-encoder--phase-profile (jj sibling on main)
- Encoder build: release, features `std parallel butteraugli-loop`
- Sweep wall: 132.8s (288 encodes)

## Tool choice

Used **inline `Instant::now()` timing markers** behind the existing
`__JXL_ENC_PHASE_TIMING` env-var pattern (mirroring what
`encode_from_precomputed` and `encode_two_pass_to_writer` already do).
No new cargo features, no `tracing` dep — just additive `let _t_X =
Instant::now()` + a single `eprintln!` summary line per encode. Default
encodes are unaffected (env var unset = zero overhead, both `_t_X` and
`_ms_X` are `let _` prefixed so Rust eliminates them entirely when
unused).

The example `examples/lossy_phase_baseline.rs` re-invokes itself as a
subprocess per (image, effort, distance) cell, sets
`__JXL_ENC_PHASE_TIMING=1`, captures stderr, parses the
`encode_inner: ... entropy=X` and `encode_two_pass: ... build_codes=X`
lines. Subprocess isolation gives clean per-encode wall-clock measurement
without warm-up bias.

Flamegraph was considered and rejected: 288 cells × per-cell SVG render
+ stack aggregation would have taken ~30 min wall and produced 288 SVGs
to summarize. The 96-cell TSV with named phases is denser and more
actionable for the adaptive-dispatch design.

## Sweep grid

- 8 images: 5 CID22-512 photos (1025469, 1044329, 1189261, 1279330, 1418519)
  + 3 GB82-SC screenshots (codec_wiki, imac_g3, terminal).
  Photos: 512×512 (0.26 MP). Screenshots: 1646×1062 / 1024×1024 / 1646×1062.
- 4 distances: 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0
- 3 efforts: 6, 7, 8
- 3 runs per cell (288 encodes total); median per phase kept

## Reproducer

```bash
cargo build -p jxl-encoder --release --features 'std parallel butteraugli-loop' \
    --example lossy_phase_baseline
./target/release/examples/lossy_phase_baseline \
    --out benchmarks/lossy_phase_baseline_2026-05-18.tsv
```

## Phase definitions (in pipeline order)

| Phase         | Code location                                                   | Description                                                                |
|---            |---                                                              |---                                                                         |
| xyb           | `convert_to_xyb_padded`                                         | Linear-sRGB → XYB SIMD, edge-replicated padding                            |
| patches       | `find_and_build_with_per_patch_gate` + `subtract_patches`       | Patch detection (post-noise, pre-gaborish)                                 |
| splines       | `find_splines_at_distance` + `subtract_splines`                 | Auto-splines (e>=7 only, gated by screenshot discriminator)                |
| quant_field   | `compute_quant_field_float_with_budget` + `quantize_quant_field` + `compute_mask1x1` | Adaptive quantization field + 1x1 mask         |
| gaborish      | `gaborish_inverse`                                              | 5x5 sharpening pre-filter (distance > 0.5)                                 |
| cfl1          | `compute_cfl_map`                                               | Per-tile chroma-from-luma pass 1 (LS, post-gaborish)                       |
| acstrat       | `compute_ac_strategy`                                           | AC strategy search (DCT8 ... DCT64x64)                                     |
| cfl2          | `refine_cfl_map`                                                | Pass 2 with actual strategies (e>=7, quant-weighted)                       |
| buttloop      | `butteraugli_refine_quant_field`                                | Butteraugli quantization loop (e>=8 only, 2-4 iters)                       |
| xform         | `transform_and_quantize`                                        | Forward DCT + quantize + nzeros count per block                            |
| sharp         | `compute_epf_sharpness`                                         | Per-block EPF sharpness selection (e>=6 dynamic, distance >= 0.5)          |
| entropy       | `encode_two_pass`                                               | Sum of subphases below                                                     |
| tok_dc        | `tokenize_dc_group*`                                            | DC + AC-metadata token collection                                          |
| co            | (custom coefficient order)                                      | Per-strategy scan order from coeff statistics                              |
| bcm           | (block context map)                                             | QF-threshold context map split                                             |
| ac_tok        | `tokenize_ac_group*`                                            | AC coefficient tokenization (parallel)                                     |
| lz77          | LZ77 backref scan                                               | RLE/greedy/optimal LZ77 (effort-dependent)                                 |
| build_codes   | `cluster_histograms` + `build_codes`                            | Histogram clustering + ANS code construction                               |
| pass2_write   | per-group entropy write                                         | Pass 2: write tokens through final codes per group                         |

## Headline numbers

Total wall-clock (mean over corpus, ms):

| effort | total ms | dominant phase (% of total)                               |
|---     |---       |---                                                        |
| e6     |    176.0 | sharp 45.5%, patches 0%, build_codes 9.2%, xform 10.1%    |
| e7     |    247.6 | sharp 33.8%, **patches 25.2%** (screenshots), bldC 5.6%   |
| e8     |    804.3 | **buttloop 71.8%**, sharp 8.5%, patches 7.2%              |

Per-megapixel (ms/MP, mean over corpus & distances):

| cls   | effort | total/MP | sharp/MP | patches/MP | acstr/MP | xform/MP | bldC/MP | buttl/MP |
|---    |---     |---       |---       |---         |---       |---       |---      |---       |
| photo | e6     |  173     |  69      |   0        |  12      |  14      |  21     |  0       |
| photo | e7     |  203     |  64      |  27        |  13      |  12      |  21     |  0       |
| photo | e8     |  579     |  55      |  27        |  13      |   9      |  19     |  **390** |
| scrn  | e6     |  106     |  48      |   0        |   8      |  11      |  10     |  0       |
| scrn  | e7     |  150     |  52      |  39        |   9      |   9      |   8     |  0       |
| scrn  | e8     |  540     |  48      |  40        |   9      |  10      |   7     |  **388** |

EPF sharpness selection (`compute_epf_sharpness`) is the surprise winner
at e6/e7 — accounts for 30-45% of wall-clock. Cost scales with distance:
0.3 ms/MP @ d=0.5 → 117 ms/MP @ d=4.0 on photos at e7. Driver: distance
>= 0.5 gate fires it on at all but the lowest-distance cells; bigger
distance → more block diversity → more sharpness candidates to score.

Butteraugli loop dominates at e8: 388-390 ms/MP across both content
classes. At d=4.0 it's 471 ms/MP on photos (worst cell). 2 iterations
each running a full forward+inverse pipeline + butteraugli comparison;
each iter is roughly the cost of a full e7 encode.

Patches at e7 are 25% of total wall-clock in the aggregate but only
because the screenshot subset dominates: per-MP photo patches are 27 ms/MP
(modest), screenshot patches are 40 ms/MP (also modest per-MP, but the
screenshots are 4-7× bigger). The aggregate 25% number is real but
content-driven.

## Top-3 adaptive-dispatch candidates

These are the highest-value (phase, content-class, effort) triples
where a content classifier could elide a large slice of wall-clock
without quality loss. Ranked by `(phase wall-clock at gated cell) ×
(probability content classifier fires correctly)`.

### #1: EPF sharpness selection at e6/e7 on smooth content (large win, easy gate)

- **Phase**: `compute_epf_sharpness` (vardct/encoder.rs:1957)
- **Cost**: 64 ms/MP on photos at e7, scales 1 ms/MP at d=0.5 →
  117 ms/MP at d=4.0; 30-45% of total wall-clock at e6 and e7
- **Adaptive gate idea**: skip per-block sharpness search when
  `mask1x1` field has low variance (smooth image) and pick the
  population mode instead. The decoder accepts any sharpness 0-7;
  picking sharpness=4 (the libjxl default when dynamic search is off)
  is a no-op for blocks where the loss curve is flat.
- **Expected savings**: 30-50 ms/MP on smooth-content e6/e7 = 30-25%
  encode time win on photo content.
- **Content classifier**: `stdev(mask1x1)` or median(mask1x1) — already
  computed for splines + screenshot detection (see
  `splines::looks_like_screenshot` for the existing
  `median(mask1x1) > 95` discriminator pattern).
- **Why now**: the existing dynamic-sharpness path is gated by
  `epf_dynamic_sharpness` (effort >= 6) but doesn't classify
  per-image. A content-aware skip would compose with the existing
  gate.

### #2: Butteraugli loop at e8 on photo content with low distance (large win, ranged gate)

- **Phase**: `butteraugli_refine_quant_field` (vardct/encoder.rs:1800)
- **Cost**: 81 ms/MP on photos @ d=0.5 at e8, scales to 471 ms/MP
  @ d=4.0; 60-75% of total wall-clock at e8
- **Adaptive gate idea**: reduce buttloop iter count from 2 → 1 (or skip
  entirely) when the initial quant field is already within bounds of
  the target distance. Use `mask1x1` statistics or the *initial*
  butteraugli score to decide whether the second iteration will move
  the needle.
- **Expected savings**: 100-200 ms/MP on `photo + d <= 1.0 + e8`
  cells, which is the bulk of e8 traffic.
- **Content classifier**: post-first-iter butteraugli delta predicts
  the second iter's marginal value. If iter 1 already converges below
  the deviation bound, skip iter 2.
- **Why now**: libjxl's buttloop is iter-count-fixed (2 @ e8, 4 @ e9+);
  early-exit is structurally cheap to add because the loop already
  recomputes global_scale per iter.

### #3: Patches detection on photo content at any effort (medium win, low risk)

- **Phase**: `find_and_build_with_per_patch_gate` (vardct/encoder.rs:1286)
- **Cost**: 27 ms/MP on photos at e7 (mostly detection scan; gating
  catches "no useful patches" but the scan ran)
- **Adaptive gate idea**: photo-class detector (variance, edge
  density, color spread) → skip the patches scan entirely. The scan
  always returns "no patches" on photos (zero overhead on patches
  output for CLIC corpus per existing CLAUDE.md notes), but the scan
  itself still costs 27 ms/MP.
- **Expected savings**: 25-30 ms/MP on every photo at e7+, which is
  10-15% of total photo encode time.
- **Content classifier**: simple "is this a photo" gate. The screenshot
  discriminator (`median(mask1x1) > 95.0`) already exists and is the
  inverse — i.e., we could `if !looks_like_screenshot { skip_patches }`
  and gain perfectly. Risk: very rare photos with text overlays would
  miss patches savings, but those are the EXACT cases the existing
  cost-benefit gate handles correctly when patches DO fire.

## Notable observations

- **Buttloop is the dominant e8 cost everywhere** (71.8% of total).
  Per-MP it's nearly content-class-invariant (388-390 ms/MP). It's a
  pixel-domain operation that doesn't care about content; the only
  knob is iter count.
- **Patches at e7 ALWAYS runs** even on photos (27 ms/MP on photos in
  this sweep) — the cost is the detection scan, not the patch ref
  frame. The existing screenshot discriminator (`median(mask1x1) > 95`)
  is the natural gate.
- **Sharpness search dominates e6/e7** at 30-45% of wall-clock. This
  is by far the biggest single lever for photo encode acceleration at
  default effort.
- `splines` is 0.0 ms across the corpus — auto-splines is correctly
  declining all screenshots (existing `looks_like_screenshot` gate
  shipped) and the photo subset stays out of the find_splines call.
- `lz77` is 0.0 at all efforts because LZ77 is gated at effort >= 7
  for VarDCT, default at e>=9, and the e8 cells use buttloop not
  LZ77.
- `cfl2` at e7 is 7 ms/MP photo vs 1 ms/MP scrn — refine_cfl_map
  cost is photo-amplified (more chroma diversity → more Newton iters).
  Small absolute cost.
- `xform` (forward DCT + quantize) is ~10-14 ms/MP across all configs
  — fairly content-invariant. Already SIMD-optimized.

## Limitations of this baseline

- Only 8 images. Cross-image variance for buttloop/sharpness could be
  10-30%; rerun on 50-image stratified subset before drawing
  source-informing constants.
- Screenshots are 4-7× bigger than photos by pixel count; cost ratios
  are normalized by MP but absolute wall-clock totals skew by class
  size. The headline `% of wall-clock` table is content-mix-dependent.
- e9 not measured. Buttloop at e9 is 4 iters (vs 2 at e8) so e9 total
  would scale roughly 2× e8 with the loop staying as the dominant
  phase.
- Single thread of subprocess but each encode uses rayon internally;
  the recorded wall-clock is parallel-encode wall-clock, not single-thread
  CPU time.
